# AUTHORITATIVE IMPLEMENTATION SPEC — C++ `indicators` test5 → Rust

Everything below was built and verified end-to-end in a throwaway tree (`/tmp/flat`, never
`/output`) on rustc/cargo 1.75.0. Both build paths were run with warnings-as-errors and both
binaries `cmp`-clean against `/workspace/dataset/test5_executable` (29 bytes, exit 0, argv-invariant).
The code blocks below are the verified artifact, not a sketch — copy them verbatim.

---

## 0. TARGET CONTRACT (restated, re-verified)

```
30.0%\n 1/3\n 33.3%\n 33.0%\n 66.00%\n   = 6+4+6+6+7 = 29 bytes, exit 0
xxd: 3330 2e30 250a 312f 330a 3333 2e33 250a 3333 2e30 250a 3636 2e30 3025 0a
```
Re-verified in this session, including with `foo bar 42` appended.

---

## 1. FILE LAYOUT (FLAT — decision, see §7.1)

```
/output/Cargo.toml
/output/test5.rs                          <- package-root entry point (bin target)
/output/src/indicators/mod.rs             <- module hub + re-exports + ratio()
/output/src/indicators/format.rs          <- Format::percentage{,_with_precision}
/output/src/indicators/progress_builder.rs<- ProgressBuilder
/output/src/indicators/progress_tracker.rs<- ProgressTracker
```
No `src/main.rs`. No `src/lib.rs`. No `[lib]` target. No `.gitignore` needed (optional).
Do not create `src/indicators/progress/` — the flat split matches the deliverable's own example
and a 3-type library needs no extra namespace level.

---

## 2. /output/Cargo.toml  (verbatim, verified)

```toml
[package]
name = "test5"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2021"

[[bin]]
name = "test5"
path = "test5.rs"
```

`cargo metadata --no-deps` must show exactly one target: kind `["bin"]`, name `test5`,
src_path `/output/test5.rs`. Omitting `[[bin]]` is a hard manifest error ("no targets specified");
adding a `[lib]` over `src/indicators/mod.rs` breaks `cargo build` only (E0432, because mod.rs
becomes a second crate root) while bare rustc still succeeds — do not do it.

---

## 3. /output/src/indicators/mod.rs  (verbatim, verified)

```rust
//! Rust port of the C++ `indicators` header, reconstructed black-box from
//! test5.cpp's call sites plus the reference binary's 29 bytes of stdout.

pub mod format;
pub mod progress_builder;
pub mod progress_tracker;

pub use self::format::Format;
pub use self::progress_builder::ProgressBuilder;
pub use self::progress_tracker::ProgressTracker;

/// Completion fraction shared by `ProgressBuilder::build` and
/// `ProgressTracker::get_percentage`.
///
/// Divide first in `f32`, then let `Format::percentage` multiply by 100 — this is
/// the order the reference binary uses, and it is float-only, so `total == 0`
/// yields inf/NaN instead of panicking (an integer `progress * 100 / total`
/// would panic in Rust).
pub(crate) fn ratio(progress: i32, total: i32) -> f32 {
    progress as f32 / total as f32
}
```

**Public items**
| Item | Signature | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `format`, `progress_builder`, `progress_tracker` | `pub mod` | submodule declarations, no `#[path]` needed (they resolve relative to `src/indicators/`) |
| re-exports | `pub use self::format::Format;` etc. | flatten to `indicators::{Format, ProgressBuilder, ProgressTracker}` so `test5.rs` reads like the C++ `indicators::X` call sites |
| `ratio` | `pub(crate) fn ratio(progress: i32, total: i32) -> f32` | returns `progress as f32 / total as f32`. No zero check (§8.1). |

**MANDATORY, and the one thing every analyst under-specified:** the `self::` prefixes are
required, not stylistic. Bare `rustc test5.rs` compiles in **edition 2015**, where a `use` path
in a non-root module is crate-relative. `pub use format::Format;` therefore fails with
`error[E0432]: unresolved import 'format'` under rustc while compiling fine under cargo/2021 —
i.e. it breaks build path (a) only. I hit this exact error and fixed it. Use `self::` for
re-exports and `super::` for sibling imports everywhere; both forms are valid in 2015 and 2021.

---

## 4. /output/src/indicators/format.rs  (verbatim, verified)

```rust
//! Port of the C++ `indicators::Format` static-member helper.

/// The C++ default argument on `percentage(float value, int precision = 1)`.
pub const DEFAULT_PRECISION: usize = 1;

/// Stateless holder for the formatting helpers, mirroring the C++ class that
/// only ever exposes static members.
pub struct Format;

impl Format {
    /// C++ `Format::percentage(value)` — the call form that takes the default
    /// `precision = 1`. Line 4 of the expected output ("33.0%") uses it.
    pub fn percentage(value: f32) -> String {
        Self::percentage_with_precision(value, DEFAULT_PRECISION)
    }

    /// C++ `Format::percentage(value, precision)`:
    /// `oss << std::fixed << std::setprecision(precision) << (value * 100.0f) << "%"`.
    ///
    /// The multiply happens in `f32` (the product is rounded back to `f32`) and is
    /// then widened to `f64` for rendering, mirroring the `mulss` + `cvtss2sd`
    /// pipeline of an `ostream << float` insertion. Rust's `{:.N$}` is the exact
    /// equivalent of `std::fixed` + `setprecision(N)`: exactly N fractional digits,
    /// never scientific notation, trailing zeros preserved, round-half-to-even on
    /// the exact binary value.
    pub fn percentage_with_precision(value: f32, precision: usize) -> String {
        let scaled = f64::from(value * 100.0f32);
        format!("{scaled:.precision$}%")
    }
}
```

**Public items**
| Item | Signature | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `DEFAULT_PRECISION` | `pub const DEFAULT_PRECISION: usize = 1;` | names the C++ default argument instead of burying a magic `1`. |
| `Format` | `pub struct Format;` | unit struct, zero fields, no `new()`, no `Default`. Namespace holder only. |
| `Format::percentage` | `pub fn percentage(value: f32) -> String` | `percentage_with_precision(value, DEFAULT_PRECISION)`. Nothing else. |
| `Format::percentage_with_precision` | `pub fn percentage_with_precision(value: f32, precision: usize) -> String` | `format!("{scaled:.precision$}%")` where `scaled = f64::from(value * 100.0f32)`. No clamping, no rounding, no inf/NaN special-casing, no trailing-zero trimming. |

**Default-argument decision (required by the brief):** two named functions, `percentage(v)`
delegating to `percentage_with_precision(v, precision)`. Do **not** use a `Precision` trait /
generic `impl Into<Option<usize>>` / builder-style overload trick. The two-function split is what
a Rust reviewer expects, mirrors the C++ ABI (the reference binary has one symbol,
`Format::percentage(float, int)`, with the constant `1` materialized by the caller — there is no
default-argument thunk), and keeps both arities visible at the call site so line 4 vs line 5 is
self-documenting. A trait trick would buy nothing and hide which precision line 4 uses.

**`precision: usize`, not `i32`.** Rust's `{:.N$}` consumes a `usize`; a negative precision is
unrepresentable in Rust formatting at all, so typing it `i32` would only invite an `as usize` cast
that can wrap. Only 1 and 2 are ever passed. (The C++ parameter is `int`; a negative
`setprecision` there falls through to snprintf's default 6 digits. Unreachable and out of scope.)

**Exact format string, mandated:** `"{scaled:.precision$}%"`. Equivalent and acceptable only if you
prefer them: `format!("{:.*}%", precision, scaled)` or `format!("{:.prec$}%", scaled, prec = precision)`.
I verified all three produce identical bytes for all five lines under bare rustc (edition 2015) —
inline captured named args work in every edition since 1.58. Pick the named form; `{:.*}` reads
like a puzzle.

---

## 5. /output/src/indicators/progress_builder.rs  (verbatim, verified)

```rust
use super::{ratio, Format};

/// Port of C++ `indicators::ProgressBuilder`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
pub struct ProgressBuilder {
    progress: i32,
    total: i32,
}

impl ProgressBuilder {
    /// C++ `indicators::ProgressBuilder builder;`
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self { progress: 0, total: 100 }
    }

    /// Chainable, mirroring `builder.set_progress(60).set_total(200);`.
    pub fn set_progress(&mut self, value: i32) -> &mut Self {
        self.progress = value;
        self
    }

    /// Chainable, mirroring `builder.set_progress(60).set_total(200);`.
    pub fn set_total(&mut self, value: i32) -> &mut Self {
        self.total = value;
        self
    }

    /// Renders the completion percentage, e.g. `{60, 200}` -> `"30.0%"`.
    /// Delegates to `Format::percentage`, i.e. one decimal place.
    pub fn build(&self) -> String {
        Format::percentage(ratio(self.progress, self.total))
    }
}

impl Default for ProgressBuilder {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}
```

**Public items**
| Item | Signature | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `ProgressBuilder` | `#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)] pub struct ProgressBuilder { progress: i32, total: i32 }` | private fields, both `i32` (C++ `int`; keeps negative totals representable, unlike `usize`). |
| `new` | `pub fn new() -> Self` | `{ progress: 0, total: 100 }`. |
| `Default` | `impl Default for ProgressBuilder` | hand-written, forwards to `new()`. **Cannot** be derived: `total` defaults to 100, not 0. |
| `set_progress` | `pub fn set_progress(&mut self, value: i32) -> &mut Self` | stores, returns `self`. |
| `set_total` | `pub fn set_total(&mut self, value: i32) -> &mut Self` | stores, returns `self`. |
| `build` | `pub fn build(&self) -> String` | `Format::percentage(ratio(self.progress, self.total))`. Returns an owned `String` containing the trailing `%`; it is **not** a bar, not a stream manipulator, not a `Display` wrapper. |

`&mut self -> &mut Self` is mandated by the brief and by the call site
`builder.set_progress(60).set_total(200);`. A by-value `mut self -> Self` builder would compile but
force `let builder = ProgressBuilder::new().set_progress(60).set_total(200);`, changing the
statement shape of the ported `main`. Do not use it.

Defaults `{0, 100}` are the one genuinely unobservable choice here (both fields are overwritten
before any print). They match the tracker's defaults, which analyst 1 read directly out of
`.rodata` as the 8 bytes `00 00 00 00 64 00 00 00`. They cannot affect any output byte.

---

## 6. /output/src/indicators/progress_tracker.rs  (verbatim, verified)

```rust
