    /// Called as a standalone statement in test5.cpp, so it mirrors C++ `void`.
    pub fn set_progress(&mut self, value: i32) {
        self.progress = value;
    }

    /// Called as a standalone statement in test5.cpp, so it mirrors C++ `void`.
    pub fn set_total(&mut self, value: i32) {
        self.total = value;
    }

    /// The stages are recorded and never read back — the C++ program stores three
    /// of them and prints none. This accessor is the only reader of the field, and
    /// nothing in a binary crate can reach it, so rustc reports it as dead code;
    /// the allow is scoped to this one item rather than muting the whole crate.
    #[allow(dead_code)]
    pub fn stages(&self) -> &[String] {
        &self.stages
    }

    /// `"{progress}/{total}"`, e.g. `{1, 3}` -> `"1/3"`.
    pub fn get_progress(&self) -> String {
        format!("{}/{}", self.progress, self.total)
    }

    /// Renders the completion percentage, e.g. `{1, 3}` -> `"33.3%"`.
    pub fn get_percentage(&self) -> String {
        Format::percentage(ratio(self.progress, self.total))
    }
}

impl Default for ProgressTracker {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}
```

**Public items**
| Item | Signature | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `ProgressTracker` | `#[derive(Debug, Clone)] pub struct ProgressTracker { progress: i32, total: i32, stages: Vec<String> }` | field order mirrors the observed layout (`progress` @+0, `total` @+4, vector @+8). Not `Copy` (owns a `Vec`). |
| `new` | `pub fn new() -> Self` | `{ progress: 0, total: 100, stages: Vec::new() }` — defaults directly observed in `.rodata`. |
| `Default` | `impl Default for ProgressTracker` | hand-written, forwards to `new()`; **not** derivable (`total = 100`). |
| `add_stage` | `pub fn add_stage(&mut self, name: &str) -> &mut Self` | `self.stages.push(name.to_string())`, returns `self`. Chainable — three chained calls at the call site prove the fluent return. `&str` is the right parameter type (the C++ takes `const std::string&`; `&str` is its idiomatic Rust counterpart and accepts the string literals directly). Do not use `impl Into<String>` / generics — needless. |
| `set_progress` | `pub fn set_progress(&mut self, value: i32)` | stores. Returns `()`. |
| `set_total` | `pub fn set_total(&mut self, value: i32)` | stores. Returns `()`. |
| `stages` | `pub fn stages(&self) -> &[String]` (with `#[allow(dead_code)]`) | returns the recorded stage names. Never called by `main`. |
| `get_progress` | `pub fn get_progress(&self) -> String` | `format!("{}/{}", self.progress, self.total)`. Plain `i32` Display on both sides of a literal `/`: no width, no padding, no separators, no percent sign, no floats. |
| `get_percentage` | `pub fn get_percentage(&self) -> String` | `Format::percentage(ratio(self.progress, self.total))` — one decimal place plus `%`. |

**Forbidden inventions:** no `current_stage`, `advance`, `next_stage`, `render`, `bar`, `width`,
`fill_char`, `Display`/`Iterator` impls, no `stage_count`, no error types. Nothing in the call
sites or the 29 bytes implies them; adding them is fabricated API.

